// PLAYTEST: does the back rank still behave, and does clearing the front
// group still feel like it accomplished something?
//
// Four groups, so two of them sit past `ENGAGED_GROUPS` and can only act
// with a `ranged` move. This is the one part of the rewrite where a wild
// program can end up with *nothing* to do — `Game::choose_wild_action`
// returns `None` and the existing "circles beyond reach" line runs — and it
// is worth seeing on a real screen rather than trusting to a unit test.
//
// The composition mixes species that have a ranged move (Drone, Zero-Day,
// Crawler) with one that does not (Scrapper), so both behaviours appear in
// the same fight depending on which group a program is standing in.
//
// What to watch:
//   - Do back-group programs that *can* shoot actually shoot, and do the
//     ones that cannot say so rather than silently skipping?
//   - Wiping the front group promotes a back group into reach. Does that
//     read as a turning point, or does the difficulty just step up with no
//     sense that you caused it?
//   - `move_ranged` is -2.56 in the shipped weights: a program that has
//     both kinds of move prefers the melee one when it is in reach. Watch
//     whether a front-group Crawler still bothers with Freeze.
(
    player: Fresh(level: 12, zone: 3),
    party: [
        (species: "scrapper", level: 9),
        (species: "sentinel", level: 9),
        (species: "sprite", level: 9),
    ],
    opponents: [
        (species: "scrapper", count: 2),
        (species: "crawler", count: 2),
        (species: "zero_day", count: 2),
        (species: "drone", count: 2),
    ],
    reps: 1,
    seed: 903,
)
